      ⍝ regression (Roy Tobin, bug-apl@gnu.org, 2026-08-04): )COPY/)LOAD of
      ⍝ a file that fails every format sniff right at offset 0 (e.g.
      ⍝ /dev/null: opens fine, but is truly zero bytes) used to leave
      ⍝ XML_Loading_Archive's tag_name/attributes/end_attr members
      ⍝ uninitialized -- next_tag() hits EOF before ever setting them,
      ⍝ but nothing checked that, so is_tag()/print_tag() went on to
      ⍝ read through the garbage pointer. Reproducibly a SEGFAULT under
      ⍝ valgrind (deterministic there: the uninitialized tag_name reads
      ⍝ back as exactly NULL) and allocator-layout-dependent without it
      ⍝ (this is why it "worked" during earlier testing but not for
      ⍝ Roy). Fixed by zero-initializing all three in the constructor
      ⍝ and guarding is_tag() against tag_name==0.
      ⍝
      ⍝ Separately: Sys.hh checked #if HAVE_MMAN_H but configure.ac only
      ⍝ ever defines HAVE_SYS_MMAN_H, so the real mmap()-based Sys::mmap()
      ⍝ had never been compiled into any build -- every build silently
      ⍝ used a read()-based fallback instead, whose 1-byte NUL-padded
      ⍝ buffer is why /dev/null previously produced "file ... does not
      ⍝ have the format ..." (a DOMAIN ERROR) rather than an outright
      ⍝ mmap() failure. With the typo fixed, mmap(fd, 0) correctly fails
      ⍝ (EINVAL: mmap()ing 0 bytes is invalid), so this now surfaces as
      ⍝ WS FULL instead -- the pre-existing, intentional error for "the
      ⍝ workspace file could not be mapped", not a new defect. The
      ⍝ file-format diagnostic itself goes to CERR (not compared here,
      ⍝ like other CERR-only warnings elsewhere in this suite); )COPY's
      ⍝ resulting error is uncaught at the harness's point of view and
      ⍝ needs ]EXPECT 1, )LOAD's does not (caught one level up, before
      ⍝ reaching immediate_execution()).
      ⍝
      )COPY /dev/null
WS FULL


      ]EXPECT 1
      )SIC

      )LOAD /dev/null

      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents


⍝ ==================================

